#: ui/pages.slint:2927
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid ""
"Your face, fingerprint or Windows PIN opens locked items on this computer, "
"in the app and when the browser extension fills a login. The key that does "
"it stays in this computer's security chip, which also slows down guessing, "
"so this is stronger than a short PIN typed by hand. The item lock's PIN or "
"password stays as the other way in, and is what you use on other devices. "
"Saving asks Windows Hello once."
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2934
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid "Save item lock…"
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2943
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid "Keyfile"
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2945
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid "On: the vault opens only with your master password and this file."
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2946
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid ""
"Off. A keyfile is a file the vault needs together with your master password."
msgstr ""

#: ui/pages.slint:2947
msgctxt "SettingsPage"
msgid ""
"Keep the keyfile on a USB stick or another place apart from the vault. "
"Someone who steals the vault file, your master password and even this "
"computer's Secret Key still can't open it without the file. The file must "
"never change, not even by one byte, and losing it (with no Recovery Key) "
"locks you out for good: keep a second copy. Any file works, but one made "
"here holds a full-strength random key. Setting or removing it turns Windows "
"Hello off until you turn it on again."
msgstr ""

